# Copy to `.env`; docker-compose.dev.yml loads it into the dev container.
#
# DATABASE_URL and NM_DATABASE_URL are NOT here on purpose: compose sets them to the `postgres`
# service, which is the only address that resolves inside the container, and anything set here
# would be overridden anyway.
#
# Append the key, do not leave it empty - the server exits at startup without it:
#   printf 'NM_SECRET_KEY=%s\n' "$(bash scripts/dev.sh cargo run -q -- keys generate | tr -d '\r')" >> .env

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The worker (`nm worker` and `nm all`; `nm serve` does not send). Defaults shown.

# The worker's own Postgres pool. It is NOT the API's, which is a fixed 20: /readyz reads an
# exhausted pool as not ready, so a worker drawing on the API's pool would let a batch of mail
# take the API down - and in `nm all` the two run in one process. The jobs loop and the campaign
# messages loop share it.
#NM_WORKER_POOL_SIZE=10

# How many messages one tick sends at once. A tick claims 16, so this is four waves, and the tick
# stops starting sends once it has used half the job lease.
#NM_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=4

# How many campaign messages are at a relay at once. A loop of its own, so a campaign never sits
# in front of a double opt-in email.
#NM_WORKER_CAMPAIGN_CONCURRENCY=32

# SENDING NEEDS AN https ORIGIN. Every message carries a one-click unsubscribe link built from
# NM_PUBLIC_URL, and a non-https one is refused - so on the default (http://localhost:8080) the
# worker refuses every confirmation email. `nm worker` and `nm all` say so at startup: a warning
# on localhost, and a refusal to start on any host subscribers could reach.
#NM_PUBLIC_URL=https://mail.example.com
